+// Whether a port declaration is one that [6.7.1] permits a net to have: "A valid
+// data type for a net shall be one of the following: a) A 4-state integral type,
+// including a packed array or packed structure. b) A fixed-size unpacked array or
+// unpacked structure, where each element has a valid data type for a net."
+//
+// A port's kind has to be decided when its symbol is created, which is before its
+// type is resolved, so this answers from the syntax alone and is deliberately
+// conservative in two ways.
+//
+// Only the built-in keyword types whose four-state-ness is lexically evident say
+// yes: a named type, a struct, a union or an enum keeps the variable default
+// rather than guessing at what it will resolve to.
+//
+// And any unpacked dimension on the declarator says no, because the dimensions are
+// what decide whether the object is a fixed-size array -- clause (b), a net -- or a
+// dynamic array, a queue or an associative array, none of which a net may be. `[]`,
+// `[$]` and `[*]` are distinguishable here, but `[n]` is not: it is a fixed size
+// when `n` is an expression and an associative index when `n` is a type, which
+// needs the name resolved. Rather than half a rule, a declarator with any unpacked
+// dimension keeps the variable default.
+//
+// Both are deviations from [23.2.2.3] for the shapes they cover, and both are
+// strictly smaller than treating every typed input port as a variable.
+//
+// Finally, the rule names "a net of default net type", so it has nothing to name
+// under `default_nettype none: the variable default stands there rather than
+// warning ImplicitNetPortNoDefault at every `input logic` in a file that opens
+// with that directive, which is a very ordinary way to write SystemVerilog.
+bool canPortBeNet(const Scope& scope, const DataTypeSyntax& type,
+                  std::span<const VariableDimensionSyntax* const> dimensions) {
+    if (!dimensions.empty() || scope.getDefaultNetType().isError())
+        return false;
+
+    switch (type.kind) {
+        case SyntaxKind::LogicType:
+        case SyntaxKind::RegType:
+        case SyntaxKind::IntegerType:
+        case SyntaxKind::TimeType:
+            return true;
+        default:
+            return false;
+    }
+}
